why 18 0 SIX_FOUR A cadential ⁶₄ on the downbeat over the bass G resolves to V: E♭ to D in the soprano, C to B in the tenor.
why 2 1/2 LEADING_TONE The tenor's B leaps up to E♭, the third of the C minor chord at the fermata. Rising to C would leave the chord without its third.
why 3 3/4 OVERLAP The tenor drops an octave, below where the bass just was, while the bass falls to D. Both move down together, so the voices stay in order.
why 6 0 OVERLAP The tune leaps up to E♭ and the alto leaps up to C with it, above where the soprano was. They move up together, so the order stays clear.
why 7 1/4 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Alto and tenor move from a fifth G/C to a twelfth C/F in contrary motion: the alto climbs while the tenor drops. Bach allows contrary fifths in inner voices; strict exercises avoid them.
why 7 1/4 SPACING The tenor drops to a low F so it can climb F–G–A♭–B♭ by step, while the alto stays high. The gap closes as the tenor rises.
why 7 3/8 SPACING The tenor is climbing back, F–G–A♭; the gap narrows with each eighth.
why 7 1/2 SPACING Still open, as the tenor climbs to A♭ and then B♭.
why 7 1/2 UNRESOLVED_NCT The alto's B♭ is a suspension that falls to A♭, while the tenor already has A♭ more than an octave below. The voices are far apart, so Bach accepts the clash.
why 7 1/2 TENDENCY The alto's B♭ does fall by step to A♭, but an eighth later: it is held over first as a suspension.
why 7 3/4 DOUBLING Read as a diminished seventh, E–G–B♭–D♭, with the alto's E arriving an eighth late, there is no ⁶₄ here and no doubling problem.
why 7 3/4 SIX_FOUR This is a diminished seventh chord (E–G–B♭–D♭) whose E arrives an eighth late: a strong pull toward F minor, not a ⁶₄ chord.
why 8 0 LEADING_TONE The alto's G is not a leading tone here: it belongs to the diminished seventh on E, and the alto steps E–F into F minor.
why 9 3/4 LEAPS The tenor rises F–B♭–E♭ in fourths through the E♭ chord to the C minor chord. Leaping through chord notes keeps the line clear.
why 11 3/4 DOUBLING Alto (B♭–C) and tenor (D–C) meet on C from opposite sides by step.
why 13 1/8 CROSSING The tenor falls C–B♭–A♭ under the bass's held C, and on the next beat the bass drops to F below it.
why 13 1/4 SIMILAR_MOTION Every voice falls into ii°⁶: soprano and alto by step, the tenor B♭–A♭ and the bass a fifth to F. The descent heads for the cadence on VI.
why 13 1/2 DOUBLING Soprano (D–C) and tenor (B♭–C) meet on C by step.
why 14 0 CROSSING Alto and tenor trade places for a beat: the alto drops to C while the tenor climbs to G, then they swap back on the F minor chord. Each line takes its next note with the least motion.
why 14 1/8 CROSSING The same swap, for one more eighth.
why 15 1/2 LEADING_TONE The alto's G moves down to E and on to F, passing through C major (V of F minor). In that key G is the fifth of V, not a leading tone.
why 15 3/4 DOUBLING The D♭ chord is VI of F minor, where the music now is; doubling its root D♭ is normal.
why 16 3/4 SPACING The tenor starts the new phrase low on F, under the bass, while the alto is high. It leaps up to D on the next beat.
why 16 3/4 CROSSING The new phrase starts in F major with the bass on A and the tenor below it on F. The tenor then leaps up to D and the voices are back in order.
why 17 1/8 LEAPS After its leap the tenor keeps climbing D–E♭–F before turning back to D. It turns, but only after reaching the top of the B♭ chord.
why 17 1/4 RANGE The bass climbs B♭–C–D with the tenor before leaping back down to E♭. A short high point.
why 17 1/2 LEAPS The bass falls D–B♭–E♭, outlining the B♭ chord before landing on E♭: roots by fifth, B♭ to E♭.
